# Authoring policy for {{PROJECT_TITLE}}

## Scope

Use the DenchCo ASD-STE100-inspired 80% house style for all new original English Wiki prose. This includes explanations, synthesis, instructions, summaries, captions, and new text in the Human and LLM Wikis. Use it from the start of a new Wiki and when adding original prose later.

80% is a pragmatic house-style aim, not a computed score or a claim of ASD-STE100 compliance. This policy is original DenchCo guidance. It does not reproduce the ASD-STE100 dictionary or replace a required formal writing standard. Follow explicit user instructions and stricter applicable local policy. Retain the project's established spelling convention.

## Core habits

- State the reader's main point early. Use familiar words when they carry the required meaning.
- Give each sentence one main idea. Aim for no more than 20 words per procedural sentence and 25 per descriptive sentence. Treat these as editing targets, not automatic pass thresholds. Retain a necessary qualification.
- Prefer active voice when the actor is known. If the evidence does not identify the actor, keep that uncertainty; do not invent one.
- Use one consistent term for one concept. Explain unfamiliar technical terms and acronyms when the reader first needs them. Keep necessary specialist vocabulary.
- Make conditions and dependencies explicit. Put a necessary condition before its action and normally give one action per procedural step. Use ordered steps for a sequence and bullets for parallel items. Keep paragraphs focused on one subject, normally within six sentences.
- Name the object of an action clearly. Replace an ambiguous pronoun only when the evidence supports the intended reference.
- Use headings that describe the content and link text that identifies its destination. Preserve governed navigation and citation formats.

## Meaning and protected text

Keep quotations, code, commands, identifiers, URLs, official names, registered source titles, units, and necessary technical terms intact. Preserve the exact canonical governing question and its required callout. Do not restyle copied source text as if it were original prose.

Retain source claims, attribution, dates, scope, uncertainty, exceptions, and evidence status. Preserve the force of normative obligations. A shorter sentence must not turn a proposal into a decision, a possibility into a fact, or an unknown responsibility into an assigned one.

## Review and exceptions

Before handing over new prose, check its meaning against the source, then review the core habits. Read adjacent text to confirm that a split sentence still has the correct scope. Existing source-link, governing-question, and navigation checks still apply. Those checks do not measure compliance with this writing style.

Record a material exception below with its affected scope and reason. Routine protected text needs no individual entry. Avoid bulk changes to existing prose unless the user requests them. This policy does not change a consumer's Standard pin or establish adoption of a later Standard revision.

| Scope | Reason for a material exception |
